pe":"text"}]}]}],"kind":"parameters"},{"content":[{"level":2,"type":"heading","text":"Overview","anchor":"overview"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"The range expression is converted to a concrete subrange relative to this","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"collection. For example, using a ","type":"text"},{"code":"PartialRangeFrom","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" range expression","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"with an array accesses the subrange from the start of the range","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"expression until the end of the array.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"type":"codeListing","syntax":"swift","code":["let streets = [\"Adams\", \"Bryant\", \"Channing\", \"Douglas\", \"Evarts\"]","let streetsSlice = streets[2...]","print(streetsSlice)","\/\/ [\"Channing\", \"Douglas\", \"Evarts\"]"]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"The accessed slice uses the same indices for the same elements as the","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"original collection uses. This example searches ","type":"text"},{"code":"streetsSlice","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" for one","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":"of the strings in the slice, and then uses that index in the original"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"array."}]},{"code":["let index = streetsSlice.firstIndex(of: \"Evarts\")    \/\/ 4","print(streets[index!])","\/\/ \"Evarts\""],"type":"codeListing","syntax":"swift"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Always use the slice’s ","type":"text"},{"code":"startIndex","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" property instead of assuming that its"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"indices start at a particular value. Attempting to access an element by"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"using an index outside the bounds of the slice’s indices may result in a"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"runtime er